module dev.slatelang.slate

import sh.sysl.gc
import sh.sysl.parsing.counted
import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}
import sysl.container.{Map, map}

// What a slate program computes with.
//
// slate is dynamically typed, so this is the tagged union every such language has, and the whole of
// its type system lives in the arms that read it.
//
// **Not one member of it is reference counted**, and that is what makes it cheap: scalars, and raw
// pointers into the collected heap. A `Value` copies with no ARC traffic, stores into GC memory
// without a write barrier, and needs no finalizer of its own -- the objects it points at have those.
//
// **An array and an object are reference types because the pointer is the value.** Two names for one
// array see each other's writes, which is what JavaScript, Python and Ruby all do and is not
// something slate implements. A number and a boolean are copied.
enum Value
    Null

    // **Absence, and the one value a program may not keep.**
    //
    // It comes from reading a field an object does not have, and nowhere else. It may be compared,
    // defaulted with `??`, and tested in a condition -- but it may not be bound to a name, stored in
    // a container, or passed to a function, and every one of those is refused where it is attempted.
    //
    // **The point is that absence stops at the boundary it arose at.** JavaScript's `undefined` is an
    // ordinary value, so it flows into a field, through three layers, and is observed a long way from
    // the lookup that made it -- by which time nothing knows what was missing. Here the program has to
    // say what absence means where it happens, which is the one place it still knows.
    Undefined
    Bool(v: bool)
    Int(v: long)
    Real(v: real)
    Str(p: *StrObj)
    Array(p: *ArrayObj)
    Object(p: *ObjectObj)
    Fn(p: *ClosureObj)

    // A value that is not there yet, which `await` waits for and an `async` function answers.
    Promise(p: *PromiseObj)

    // `a..b` and `a..<b`.
    //
    // **All scalars, so a range needs no object in the collected heap** -- which is the reason it is
    // a variant here rather than a `*RangeObj`: two numbers and three flags copy as cheaply as an
    // integer does, and nothing about a range can cycle.
    //
    // `has_lo` and `has_hi` say which ends were written. An end left out is taken from whatever the
    // range is used on -- zero and the length, for a slice -- and a `for` over a range with no end
    // is refused rather than run forever.
    RangeVal(lo: long, hi: long, inclusive: bool, has_lo: bool, has_hi: bool)

    // A TCP socket -- a listener, or a connection at either end of one.
    //
    // **A slot in the table `event.sysl` keeps, and not the handle itself.** A `&Tcp` is a counted
    // box and a counted box may not live in the collected heap at all (see `obj.sysl`), so what a
    // program holds is the slot and what holds the handle is the slot. That also makes a socket a
    // scalar, which is what keeps this union free of reference counting.
    //
    // **`gen` is why this is two numbers and not one.** A spent slot is reused, so `id` alone names
    // a position rather than a socket: a value held across a `close` would come to mean whatever
    // took the position next, and compare equal to it. The generation is stamped at the claim and
    // never reused, so a closed socket's value matches nothing ever again.
    Socket(id: usize, gen: usize)

    // A compiled regular expression -- a slot in the table `regex.sysl` keeps, and not the pattern
    // itself. A `&pcre2.Regex` is a counted box, and one may not live in the collected heap.
    //
    // **One number where a socket needs two**, and the difference says what the two tables are. A
    // socket's slot is surrendered by `close` and handed to the next socket, so its value carries a
    // generation to keep a closed socket from coming to mean a live one. A compiled pattern is never
    // released -- there is no `close` for one and a program cannot ask -- so a slot names the same
    // pattern for as long as the program runs, and the id alone is the whole of the value.
    RegexVal(id: usize)

    // A declared type, as a value -- `type NewNote = { ... }` binds one under its own name.
    //
    // **A slot in the table `shape.sysl` keeps, and one number for `RegexVal`'s reason**: a shape is
    // never released, so a slot names the same shape for as long as the program runs. What the slot
    // holds is the resolved pattern, the name it was declared under, and **the scope it was declared
    // in** -- the last so that a shape naming a class still finds that class wherever the shape is
    // carried to. A pattern is a counted box and a scope is a collected one, so neither may sit in a
    // `Value` and both sit in the table.
    //
    // **The shape itself stays opaque: nothing reads its structure back out.** A program may ask
    // whether a value fits and what does not fit about it, and that is the whole of the surface --
    // which is what keeps "a type has no run-time cost" true in the sense that mattered.
    Shape(id: usize)

    // A point on the timeline, in microseconds from 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z.
    //
    // **It names no place and no calendar, which is the whole reason there are eight of these and
    // not one.** JavaScript has a single `Date`, which is an instant that answers calendar questions
    // by silently reading the host's zone -- so `d.getFullYear()` gives a different answer on the
    // developer's machine and on the server, and nothing in the program says which zone it meant.
    // An instant here has no year until a zone is named.
    InstantVal(us: long)

    // An **exact** length of timeline, in microseconds.
    //
    // Not a number of days: a day a zone's clocks move through is 23 or 25 hours long, so a length
    // that means "a day" is a `PeriodVal` and a length that means 24 hours is this. Which one a
    // program wanted is the difference between two right answers, so it is a type rather than a
    // parameter.
    DurationVal(us: long)

    // A calendar day, as a count from 1970-01-01. No time of day and no zone.
    DateVal(day: int)

    // A wall clock reading, in microseconds from midnight. No date and no zone.
    //
    // **It has no arithmetic**, which is deliberate: 23:30 plus an hour is either 00:30 the next day
    // or a fault, and nothing about a bare reading says which was meant. Combine it with a date and
    // the question answers itself.
    TimeVal(us: long)

    // A civil reading -- a date and a time of day -- with no zone.
    //
    // What a person writes on a form and what a calendar entry holds before anybody says where it
    // happens. It names a different instant in every zone, and in one zone twice a year it names
    // either two instants or none.
    DateTimeVal(day: int, us: long)

    // A zone: a region's rules, or a fixed offset from UTC.
    //
    // **A slot in the table `time.sysl` keeps, and not the rules themselves**, for `Socket`'s reason
    // -- a parsed zone is a struct of counted sequences and a counted box may not live in the
    // collected heap. A zone is interned by name and never released, so unlike a socket there is
    // nothing to reuse and no generation to stamp.
    ZoneVal(id: usize)

    // An instant read in a zone: the type an appointment, a log line and a calendar entry all want.
    //
    // Both halves are needed and neither is enough. The instant is what orders two of them and what
    // a difference is taken between; the zone is what makes it 9 in the morning rather than a number
    // of microseconds, and what makes tomorrow morning still 9 in the morning after the clocks move.
    ZonedVal(us: long, zone: usize)

    // A **calendar** length: whole months and whole days.
    //
    // **The days are not months and never convert**, which is the fact this type exists to keep.
    // A month is 28, 29, 30 or 31 days depending on which one and which year, so `months(1).days()`
    // is zero rather than a guess -- and adding a month to the 31st of January lands on the last day
    // of February rather than on the 3rd of March, which is where JavaScript's `setMonth` puts it.
    PeriodVal(months: int, days: int)

    // A generator: a line of execution a program drives itself, one `yield` at a time.
    //
    // **A slot in the table `generator.sysl` keeps, for `Socket`'s reason** -- what it holds is a
    // parked `Machine`, whose stack and frames are counted buffers that may not live in the
    // collected heap at all. The machine itself sits in `parked`, so the tracer already walks
    // everything the suspended body is holding.
    //
    // **`gen` is here for the reason a socket's is**: a generator that has run to the end gives its
    // slot back, so a bare id would come to mean whatever took the slot next -- and a finished
    // generator would compare equal to a fresh one.
    Gen(id: usize, gen: usize)

    // A function the interpreter supplies.
    //
    // **A closed enum rather than a name, so that a `Value` stays free of reference counting.** A
    // `string` here would be the one ARC member in the union and would put a finalizer on every
    // object that can hold a value.
    Native(f: NativeFn)

    // The name a program uses for this kind of value, which is what a runtime complaint says.
    type_name(self) -> string = self match
        Null -> "null"
        Undefined -> "undefined"
        Bool(_) -> "a boolean"
        Int(_) -> "an integer"
        Real(_) -> "a real"
        Str(_) -> "a string"
        Array(_) -> "an array"
        Object(_) -> "an object"
        Fn(_) -> "a function"
        Promise(_) -> "a promise"
        RangeVal(_, _, _, _, _) -> "a range"
        Socket(_, _) -> "a socket"
        RegexVal(_) -> "a regular expression"
        Shape(_) -> "a shape"
        InstantVal(_) -> "an instant"
        DurationVal(_) -> "a duration"
        DateVal(_) -> "a date"
        TimeVal(_) -> "a time"
        DateTimeVal(_, _) -> "a dateTime"
        ZoneVal(_) -> "a zone"
        ZonedVal(_, _) -> "a zoned"
        PeriodVal(_, _) -> "a period"
        Gen(_, _) -> "a generator"
        Native(_) -> "a function"

    // Whether this counts as true in a condition.
    //
    // **Only `false` and `null` are false, and nothing else is.** The alternative -- zero and the
    // empty string being false as well -- is the rule JavaScript and Python took and is the one most
    // often written about as a mistake: it makes `if xs.len` and `if xs.len != 0` differ from each
    // other in a way nobody predicts. slate takes Ruby's and Lua's side of that argument.
    truthy(self) -> bool = self match
        Null -> false
        Undefined -> false
        Bool(v) -> v
        _ -> true
end Value

// The functions a program has without writing them.
enum NativeFn derives Display
    NPrint
    NAssert
    NAssertEq
    NLen

    // -- text --
    NChars
    NSplit
    NJoin
    NContains
    NIndexOf
    NLastIndexOf
    NStartsWith
    NEndsWith
    NTrim
    NTrimStart
    NTrimEnd
    NUpper
    NLower
    NReplace
    NRepeat

    // -- numbers --
    NNumber
    NInteger
    NReal
    NBoolean
    NAbs
    NFloor
    NCeil
    NRound
    NTrunc
    NSqrt
    NPow
    NMin
    NMax
    NPush
    NString
    NKeys
    NParseJson
    NToJson
    NToBytes
    NFromBytes
    NValues
    NEntries
    NHas

    // -- the object model every value answers to --
    //
    // **These four are the universal methods**, reached on any value that has not put a field of the
    // same name in front of them. `method.sysl` says why they are the only names allowed to do that.
    NToString
    NEq
    NNe
    NEquals

    // -- arrays --
    //
    // **The participle answers a new array and the bare verb changes the one it was given**, which
    // is Python's convention and is what makes a mutation visible at the call site. `array.sysl`
    // holds the rest of the rule.
    NPop
    NInsert
    NRemoveAt
    NClear
    NMap
    NFilter
    NReduce
    NFind
    NFindIndex
    NEvery
    NSome
    NSort
    NSorted
    NReverse
    NReversed
    NConcat
    NFlat
    NSum

    // The eight an array answers that it did not before. **`at` and `slice` count from the END where
    // the index is negative**, which is the whole reason JavaScript grew `at` beside `xs[i]`.
    NSlice
    NShift
    NUnshift
    NFlatMap
    NForEach
    NFindLast
    NFindLastIndex

    // The event loop's, which are node's names because a program that knows node should not have to
    // learn new ones for the same thing.
    NSetTimeout
    NSetInterval
    NClearTimeout

    // Promises. `sleep` is the one that makes `await` worth having on its own -- everything else a
    // program can build out of an async function.
    NSleep
    NResolve
    NReject

    // The file system. **node's names**, and every one of them answers a promise -- there is no
    // blocking form, which is the one place slate is stricter than node rather than looser.
    NReadFile
    NReadBytes
    NWriteFile
    NAppendFile
    NStderr
    NReadDir
    NStat
    NExists
    NRemove
    NMkdir
    NRmdir
    NRename

    // The same ten, blocking. **`Sync` is node's suffix and the reason for having them is node's
    // too**: a program that reads its configuration before it starts, or a script whose whole job is
    // three file operations in order, gains nothing from a promise and pays for it in ceremony.
    NReadFileSync
    NReadBytesSync
    NWriteFileSync
    NAppendFileSync
    NReadDirSync
    NStatSync
    NExistsSync
    NRemoveSync
    NMkdirSync
    NRmdirSync
    NRenameSync

    // TCP. **A socket is a handle with a lifetime**, which is what makes this different from the
    // file system: `connect` and `send` answer promises, and `listen` and `onData` take callbacks,
    // because a stream is a run of events rather than one answer.
    NListen
    NConnect
    NOnData
    NOnBytes
    NOnError
    NSend
    NCloseSocket
    NLocalPort
    NStartTls

    // **Which application protocol a TLS connection settled on**, which is the one question a server
    // taking both HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 on one port cannot answer any other way.
    NAlpnProtocol

    // Dates and times. **One word per question, and the receiver says which question it is.**
    // `2.hours()` makes a length and `d.hours()` asks how many whole hours a length is; `date(y, m,
    // d)` builds a calendar day and `date(z)` reads one off a zoned reading. That is the same rule
    // `method.sysl` already runs on -- a builtin method is the free function with the receiver in
    // front -- applied to a vocabulary where the noun and the verb are the same word in English too.
    NNow
    NMonotonic
    NDate
    NTime
    NDateTime
    NZone
    NLocalZone
    NInstant
    NEpochSeconds
    NEpochMillis
    NEpochMicros

    // The exact lengths and the calendar ones, which are two scales rather than one. Nothing here
    // converts between them, because a month is not a number of days and a day is not a number of
    // hours.
    NMicros
    NMillis
    NSeconds
    NMinutes
    NHours
    NDays
    NWeeks
    NMonths
    NYears

    // What a reading is made of. Singular where the length words are plural, which is the whole of
    // how `year(d)` is told from `years(3)`.
    NYear
    NMonth
    NDay
    NHour
    NMinute
    NSecond
    NWeekday
    NMonthName
    NDayOfYear
    NDaysInMonth
    NIsLeapYear
    NAt
    NOffset
    NIsDst
    NAbbrev
    NStartOf
    NOnOrAfter
    NFormat
    NParseDate
    NParseTime
    NParseDateTime
    NParseTimestamp

    // Another program, run and read. **On libuv like every other host binding here**, slate being an
    // asynchronous language and `sysl.process` being blocking throughout.
    NRun
    NEnv

    // Stopping the program with a status of the program's own choosing. **`args` is beside it in
    // `slate:process` and is not here**, being a value rather than a call: the arguments are fixed
    // before the first statement runs, so asking for them cannot fail and cannot change its answer.
    NExit

    // Being asked to stop by something outside the program, which is the other half of `exit` and
    // the only way a server ever ends. In `slate:process` for the same reason `exit` is: what this
    // process is told and what it tells the shell are one subject.
    NOnSignal
    NOffSignal

    // A generator, driven a step at a time. `for x in g` is the other way to drive one and needs no
    // builtin -- `IterNext` asks the same question the loop already asks of an array.
    NGenNext

    // An HTTP server, on the event loop slate already has, and the request a program makes itself.
    NFetch

    // **A promise nobody has answered yet, and the two ways of answering it.** `resolve` and
    // `reject` make a promise that has already settled, which is the easy half; these are the half
    // that bridges a callback into `await`, and without them a program can only wait for something
    // slate itself started. TC39's `Promise.withResolvers` is the same three pieces.
    NPending
    NSettle
    NFail

    // **The parser `slate:http` reads bytes with, and `slate:llhttp` is the door to it.** They are
    // declared inside `if everything`, so they are not in a program's own scope -- but the export
    // list in `stdlib.sysl` hands them out, which is what lets a proxy, a client or a protocol that
    // starts as HTTP be written as a package rather than as a change to slate.
    NHttpParser
    NHttpStream
    NHttpFeed
    NHttpTake
    NHttpTakePart
    NHttpFinish
    NHttpClose
    NHttpUpgraded

    // **HTTP/2, which is `slate:nghttp2` and is nghttp2 underneath.** The sibling of the eight above and
    // public for their reason: this is the framing and header-compression layer, and what is written
    // over it -- a server taking both versions on one port, a client that keeps its connections, a
    // proxy, gRPC -- is a package rather than a change to slate.
    NH2Client
    NH2Server
    NH2Receive
    NH2Send
    NH2Next
    NH2Request
    NH2Respond

    // The same two with the body left open, and the two that write it. **A streamed body is four
    // names rather than a flag on the other two**, because what a caller does afterwards is
    // different: it holds the stream and writes to it, where `h2Respond` is finished when it
    // returns.
    NH2RequestStream
    NH2RespondStream
    NH2Push
    NH2Finish

    NH2Settings
    NH2Goaway
    NH2Reset
    NH2WindowUpdate
    NH2Consume
    NH2Ping
    NH2Wants
    NH2Close

    // **HPACK on its own, which needs no session at all.** It is the security-relevant half of
    // HTTP/2 and is identical whoever drives the frames, so a program holding a header block from
    // anywhere -- a capture, a test vector, a proxy's log -- can read it without one.
    NHpackDeflater
    NHpackInflater
    NHpackDeflate
    NHpackInflate
    NHpackClose

    // **What `slate:jwt` signs and checks with**, and inside `if everything` for the same reason the
    // parser is: a token's shape is slate's business and the cryptography is not part of the
    // language.
    NJwsSign
    NJwsVerify

    // **What `slate:crypto` hands a PROGRAM**, which is a different question from what the two
    // modules above stand on: a package cannot have a native, so anything installed with `slate add`
    // had no digest and -- the half that cannot be written in slate at any price -- no randomness.
    // `NSha1` is in both lists, `slate:ws` reaching it from the builtin scope and a program by name.
    NMd5
    NSha1
    NSha256
    NSha384
    NSha512
    NHmac
    NPbkdf2
    NRandomBytes
    NTimingSafeEqual

    // **What `slate:password` hashes with.** Not inside `if everything`: these three ARE the module,
    // rather than a seam a module written in slate stands on, so they are declared the way
    // `slate:regex`'s one name is and the export list takes them out of the builtin scope.
    NPasswordHash
    NPasswordHashStrong
    NPasswordCheck
    NPasswordNeedsRehash

    // **Brotli, which `slate:brotli` exports and `slate:http` uses without being asked.** Declared
    // like the password natives rather than inside `if everything`: they ARE the module, and the
    // server reaching for them is a second consumer of the same two names rather than a seam.
    NBrotliCompress
    NBrotliDecompress

    // **gzip and zlib, which are `slate:gzip`.** Beside brotli rather than inside `if everything`
    // for its reason -- these four ARE the module. What separates them from brotli is that a
    // JavaScript host has them: `CompressionStream` speaks both wrappers and speaks no brotli, so
    // this is the compression a program has wherever slate runs.
    NGzip
    NGunzip
    NDeflate
    NInflate

    // **What `slate:redis` recognises RESP with**, inside `if everything` for the reason the HTTP
    // parser is: a reply's shape is slate's business and the byte scan is not part of the language.
    NRedisReader
    NRedisFeed
    NRedisTake
    NRedisCommand
    NRedisClose

    // **What the host this program is running on provides ITSELF**, and the six calls that reach the
    // one such thing a browser has and the interpreter does not. `hostHas` is the question a built-in
    // module asks before deciding which of two implementations of one surface to use; the rest are
    // the browser's `WebSocket`, which is a client a page can open where it has no socket at all.
    NHostHas
    NWsOpen
    NWsSend
    NWsSendBytes
    NWsOnMessage
    NWsOnClose
    NWsClose

    // Regular expressions, on PCRE2. **The names a regex answers to are its own** -- `NReReplace` is
    // not `NReplace` and `NReSplit` is not `NSplit`, because a method is looked up by the kind of its
    // receiver and the two do different things to different arguments. Sharing a variant would give
    // one diagnostic for two calls.
    NRegex
    NReTest
    NReFind
    NReFindAll
    NReReplace
    NReReplaceFirst
    NReSplit
    NRePattern
    NReFlags

    // What a declared type answers to, once it is a value. **`test` is deliberately the same word a
    // regular expression answers to**, both being the same question asked of the two things in slate
    // that describe a set of values -- and `mismatch` is the one a regular expression has no use for,
    // there being nothing inside a subject to point at.
    NShapeTest
    NShapeMismatch
    NShapeName

    // **The document, which is `slate:dom` and exists only where there IS one.** Every one of these
    // faults under the interpreter, saying so in as many words: slate runs in three places and only
    // one of them has a page. They are here rather than absent for the reason the JavaScript back
    // end's `OWED` list exists on the other side of the same seam -- a name that says what it needs
    // is worth more than a name that is not there.
    //
    // **A node is a slate INTEGER and not the engine's object**, which is the rule the timers
    // already run on: the engine keeps a table and hands out the number, so nothing foreign ever
    // becomes a slate value and `==`, `print` and every pattern keep working on what a program
    // holds.
    NDomCreateElement
    NDomCreateText
    NDomSetAttribute
    NDomRemoveAttribute
    NDomSetProperty
    NDomOn
    NDomOff
    NDomSetChildren
    NDomSetText
    NDomById
    NDomQuery
    NDomMarkup
    NDomRelease
end NativeFn

// The text of a slate string, for the interpreter's own use.
text_of(p: *StrObj) -> string = p.s

// A slate string value over a sysl string. The GC object becomes that string's only owner.
str_value(s: string) -> Value = Str(new_str(s))

// -- scopes -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// What a name holds, looked up outward through the chain.
lookup(e: *EnvObj, name: string) -> Option[Value]
    var scope = e

    // A loop rather than the recursion this reads as, because a scope chain is as deep as a
    // program's nesting and a walk up it is a walk up a linked list.
    loop
        if scope == null then return None

        scope.names.get(name) match
            Some(v) -> return Some(v)
            None -> scope = scope.parent
end lookup

// Whether a name is bound anywhere in the chain.
knows(e: *EnvObj, name: string) -> bool = lookup(e, name).is_some()

// Whether a name is bound in THIS scope, without looking outward.
//
// **The distinction is what a default parameter turns on.** A call's scope holds exactly the
// arguments that were given, so asking about this scope alone answers "was it given"; asking the
// chain would find a global of the same name and answer yes to a parameter nobody passed.
bound_here(e: *EnvObj, name: string) -> bool = e.names.has(name)

// Bind a name in this scope, shadowing whatever an outer one had.
declare(e: *EnvObj, name: string, v: Value, assignable: bool)
    e.names.put(name, v)
    e.mutable.put(name, assignable)

// Write to an existing binding, wherever in the chain it lives.
//
// Three answers rather than two: the name may be unknown, or known and not writable, and the two are
// different complaints.
assign_name(e: *EnvObj, name: string, v: Value) -> Assigned
    var scope = e

    loop
        if scope == null then return Unknown

        if scope.names.has(name)
            if !scope.mutable.get_or(name, false) then return Immutable

            scope.names.put(name, v)
            return Written
        end if

        scope = scope.parent
end assign_name

// What happened when a name was written to.
enum Assigned
    Written
    Immutable
    Unknown

// The field a data variant's maker carries its own name under.
//
// **Unspellable on purpose**, as a constructor's hidden binding is: it sits on the maker rather than
// on the value, so an instance finds it up the proto chain and pays nothing for it, and no program
// can read or write it by name. It exists so that a data value can print as what made it -- an
// object cannot otherwise say which variant it is, the maker not knowing its own name either.
const DataTag: string = "(data)"

// The name a CLASS declaration writes on the class object, by which a value made from it knows what
// it is.
//
// **The same arrangement `DataTag` uses, for the same two reasons**: it sits on the class rather
// than on the instance, so an instance finds it up the proto chain, carries nothing itself, and it
// stays out of `keys`, out of `==` and out of a `with` copy. It is unspellable, so no program can
// forge one.
//
// **It exists because nothing downstream of the parser knew that classes existed**, which was right
// while a class was sugar for an object and stopped being right the day a class instance got an
// equality of its own. `class_name` reads it and two questions rest on the answer: whether `==` on
// this value means identity, and what name its `toString` prints.
const ClassTag: string = "(class)"

// The field a `class` or `data` declaration writes its own SHAPE under.
//
// **The third of the unspellable tags, and it is on the same terms as the other two**: it sits on
// the class or the variant maker, out of `keys`, out of `==`, out of a `with` copy and out of what a
// program can write. An instance reaches it up the chain and pays nothing for it.
//
// **It is what makes a class name answer `test`, `mismatch` and `name`**, which the types page has
// always said a class name is -- `docs/reference/types.md` lists "a `type`, `class` or `data` name"
// as a type expression, and until this the value half of those two was a plain object with no shape
// near it. `read_field` reaches this only where the object's own fields and its whole proto chain
// have said nothing, so a static the program itself declared wins.
const ShapeTag: string = "(shape)"

// Whether a key is one of the two tags a declaration wrote rather than a field a program did.
//
// **A tag is not a field and nothing that enumerates fields may report one.** `keys`, `values`,
// `entries` and `len` all walk the table's own entries, so without this a class object answers
// `["(class)", "new"]` to `keys` -- a name no program wrote, cannot have written, and would have to
// filter out of every walk over a class. `print` has hidden them from the start and these four had
// not caught up.
//
// Not `private`: the three walks are in `table.sysl`, `array.sysl` and `builtin.sysl`, and three
// copies of one two-name list is how one of them comes to disagree.
tag_key(k: Value) -> bool = k match
    Str(p) -> p.s == DataTag || p.s == ClassTag || p.s == ShapeTag
    _ -> false

// What to say when a call gives the wrong number of arguments.
//
// **A function with a default parameter takes a RANGE**, and saying only the upper end of it would
// send a reader looking for the argument they left out on purpose. Where there is no default the
// two ends are the same number and the sentence is the one slate has always said.
// **A REST PARAMETER HAS NO UPPER END AT ALL**, so a call cannot give too many and the only thing
// left to say is the floor. Naming an upper end there would be naming a number the function does not
// have.
arity_complaint(required: usize, takes: usize, given: usize, variadic: bool) -> string
    if variadic
        return s"this function takes at least ${counted(required, "argument")} and was given ${given}"

    if required == takes
        return s"this function takes ${counted(takes, "argument")} and was given ${given}"

    s"this function takes from ${required} to ${counted(takes, "argument")} and was given ${given}"

// What to say when an object is called and has no `new` to call.
//
// **The reader has to be told that calling an object is a real thing before being told this one
// cannot be**, or the sentence reads as "objects are not callable" and sends them to rewrite a call
// that was right. What is wrong is the object, which is missing the field the call would have used.
no_maker() -> string =
    "an object is called only where it has a `new`, and this one has none -- `class` and `data` write one, and an ordinary object is data rather than something to call"
